  "sourceAndTextContext": {
    "independentSourcePixelObservationsScopedToLabel": [
      "The source shows one shared horizontal axis passing through O1, A, O, B, and O2, with two curved lens boundaries and two oblique radius segments R1 and R2.",
      "Principal axis is an English explanatory caption added in the modern figure, not a recovered source label. The source has no second horizontal axis for this caption to identify.",
      "This review checks the caption position and attribution only. It does not resolve the retained interior-mark or heavy-arrow-stroke uncertainties, judge the regularized fill, or reapprove the finite lens geometry."
    ],
    "authoritativeTextLocation": "source.authoritativeText in the hash-pinned candidate/spec.json; relevant bilingual passages only. No DB or live lesson was accessed.",
    "relevantTextEntries": [
      {
        "jsonPointer": "/source/authoritativeText/3",
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n75",
        "text": "EN /prose/3/text\nLenses are commonly made of glass.\nThe line $O_1O_2$ through the centers of curvature of the two spherical surfaces is the **principal axis**, as in Figure 164.\nHere $O_1$ and $O_2$ are centers of curvature, not the optical center defined below."
      },
      {
        "jsonPointer": "/source/authoritativeText/8",
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n75",
        "text": "EN /prose/12/text\nA **thin lens** is the simplified model used in this section.\nA lens is called a **thin lens** when its thickness $l=|AB|$ in Figure 164 is negligible compared with the radii of curvature $R_1$, $R_2$ and the object-to-lens distance.\nNegligible thickness is an approximation, not a claim that a real lens has no thickness.\nAll lenses discussed below use this thin-lens model."
      },
      {
        "jsonPointer": "/source/authoritativeText/10",
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n75",
        "text": "EN /prose/14/text\nIn the thin-lens approximation, the two surface vertices $A$ and $B$ are sufficiently close to be treated as a single point.\nThis approximately coincident point is the **optical center**, denoted by $O$."
      },
      {
        "jsonPointer": "/source/authoritativeText/11",
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n75",
        "text": "EN /prose/15/text\nThe principal axis of a thin lens passes through its optical center.\nAny other line through that center is a **secondary optical axis**."
      },
      {
        "jsonPointer": "/source/authoritativeText/35",
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n75",
        "text": "ZH /prose/3/text\n透镜常用玻璃制成。\n通过两个球面曲率中心的直线 $O_1O_2$ 叫做透镜的**主光轴**，如图 164 所示。\n这里的 $O_1$ 和 $O_2$ 是球面的曲率中心，不是后面定义的光心。"
      },
      {
        "jsonPointer": "/source/authoritativeText/40",
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n75",
        "text": "ZH /prose/12/text\n**薄透镜**是本节采用的简化模型。\n图 164 中的厚度 $l=|AB|$，与两个球面的曲率半径 $R_1$、$R_2$ 以及物体到透镜的距离相比，小到可以忽略时，透镜就称为**薄透镜**。\n可以忽略是近似条件，不是说真实透镜没有厚度。\n以下所说的透镜都采用这一薄透镜模型。"
      },
      {
        "jsonPointer": "/source/authoritativeText/42",
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n75",
        "text": "ZH /prose/14/text\n在薄透镜近似中，两个球面的顶点 $A$ 和 $B$ 足够接近，可以把它们当作同一个点。\n这个近似重合的点叫做**透镜的光心**，用 $O$ 表示。"
      },
      {
        "jsonPointer": "/source/authoritativeText/43",
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n75",
        "text": "ZH /prose/15/text\n薄透镜的主光轴通过光心。\n通过光心的其他任何一条直线叫做**副光轴**。"
      }
    ],
    "meaningUsedForLabelAttribution": "Unchanged bilingual prose defines the principal axis as the line through the two curvature centers O1/O2, distinguishes those centers from O, and identifies l = |AB| separately as lens thickness. These distinguish the caption from the radius and thickness annotations.",
